detail.abstract:
To understand whether artificial intelligence will alter long-run growth, we first need to understand why growth rates remained nearly constant for 200 years, despite changes in virtually all presumed determinants. This paper proposes an answer based on an empirical regularity overlooked by economists: science and technology have grown at a constant rate not just since 1820 (like GDP and population) but since 1720, a full century earlier. This "new Kaldor fact" suggests that knowledge growth has been independent of economic and demographic inputs, challenging established growth theory. Building on cognitive and network science, I explain the finding with an idea-network model where combinatorial growth is bounded by human cognitive limitations (in our capacity to process, associate, and combine complex ideas) that determine the steady rate of techno-scientific and economic growth. Artificial intelligence, potentially free of these limitations, may be about to dissolve the three-century-old regime of steady-rate idea growth.&nbsp;
